gration; RayJob via the kuberay integration — validate and close any gaps).\n- Configuration defaults and admin-facing documentation of how quotas are set per project.\n- Incidental documentation updates that fall out of this change (quota configuration references) are included here; no separate docs work.\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n- A job whose project has exhausted its GPU quota is not admitted (remains pending) rather than partially scheduled — for both PyTorchJob and RayJob (HLR-2).\n- Quota consumption accounts for GPU resources across both training job types within a project.\n- Demonstrated test: over-quota job stays queued while quota is held by admitted jobs; on release, the pending job is admitted.\n- Per-project quota is configurable by an administrator without code changes.\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-2 (P0) — full coverage.\n\n---\n\n**E004 body** (preemption semantics investigation):\n\nTitle: Determine required preemption semantics and checkpoint overhead for training workloads\n\nDescription: Resolves the strategy's open question on preemption semantics (HLR-4): whether reference customers need full eviction with requeue, or checkpoint-aware graceful preemption. Conflicting field signals exist and no benchmark of checkpoint overhead exists. Produces a decision plus a measured checkpoint-overhead benchmark to ground it.\n\nScope:\n- Benchmark checkpoint overhead for representative multi-node PyTorchJob training workloads: checkpoint save time, restart/restore time, and impact on job progress, at realistic job sizes.\n- Consolidate field signals on required preemption semantics from reference customers.\n- Produce a recommendation with two bounded outcomes: (A) full eviction with requeue (simple Kueue preemption policy), or (B) checkpoint-aware graceful preemption (queueing-side graceful preemption plus training-side checkpoint hooks).\n- Define the admin-defined preemption policy requirements (priority scheme, which workloads are preemptable).\n\nAcceptan