{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8a3aa335d01b0a58","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9005\n3\tscore: 11\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: critical\n8\t  criterion: DAG Coherence\n9\t  description: 'Summary DAG diagram omits the E004 -> BRANCH-B-E006 edge: BRANCH-B-E006\n10\t    frontmatter lists RHAISTRAT-9005-E004 in dependencies (required as its gated_by\n11\t    target) and the DAG Justification table records the gate, but the mermaid diagram\n12\t    draws only B005 --> B006. Every other gate edge (E001 -> branches, E004 -> A005/B005,\n13\t    E007 -> A008) is drawn. Frontmatter is correct; add E004 --> B006 to the diagram.'\n14\t- severity: minor\n15\t  criterion: Completeness\n16\t  description: E004 acceptance criterion 1 permits a third bounded-hybrid outcome\n17\t    (admin-selectable per queue), but the conditional-branch structure models exactly\n18\t    two mutually exclusive outcomes (A/B); a hybrid decision has no corresponding\n19\t    branch plan. Tighten the AC to select outcome A or B per the strategy binary framing,\n20\t    or add a hybrid branch.\n21\terror: null\n22\t---\n23\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9005\n24\t\n25\t## Review Summary\n26\t\n27\tScore: 11/14 — fail\n28\tRecommendation: revise\n29\t\n30\tFull (non-triaged) decomposition of a Critical-priority strategy spanning three\n31\tcomponents, two teams, and five HLRs (P0–P2) with three open questions. The\n32\tdecomposition is structurally strong — complete HLR traceability, well-formed\n33\tconditional branches, correct gating metadata, and type-appropriate AI signal\n34\tsets — but fails on one DAG coherence defect: the summary's mermaid diagram\n35\tomits the E004 → BRANCH-B-E006 gate edge that exists in the epic frontmatter.\n36\t\n37\t## Criterion Details\n38\t\n39\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n40\t\n41\tAll five HLRs are covered. HLR-1 (P0): E001 investigation + mutually exclusive\n42\tBRANCH-A/B-E003 implementations. HLR-2 (P0): E002. HLR-3 (P1): E006. HLR-4\n43\t(P1): E004 investigation + BRANCH-A-E005 or BRANCH-B-E005 + BRANCH-B-E006.\n44\tHLR-5 (P2): E007 + BRANCH-A-E008, with branch B an explicit, justified deferral\n45\t(Tech Preview maturity risk, contained by tiered delivery). The traceability\n46\tmatrix is present in the summary and every epic carries an HLR Traceability\n47\tsection. Priority inheritance is correct throughout: each Investigation's\n48\tpriority matches the HLR it gates (E001/P0 → P0 branches, E004/P1 → P1\n49\tbranches, E007/P2 → P2 branch), and E002 (P0) → E006 (P1) is a valid\n50\tdowngrade. No priority collapse: each epic maps to a single HLR, so deferrable\n51\tP2 work (topology) sits in its own gated epic. Triage is null (full\n52\tdecomposition), so multi-epic output is expected.\n53\t\n54\t### 2. DAG Coherence (0/2)\n55\t\n56\t**Critical — diagram/frontmatter mismatch.** BRANCH-B-E006's frontmatter lists\n57\t`RHAISTRAT-9005-E004` in `dependencies` (correctly — E004 is its `gated_by`\n58\ttarget and must appear in direct dependencies), and the DAG Justification\n59\ttable records \"E004 ⟶ BRANCH-A/B-E005, BRANCH-B-E006 (gate)\", but the mermaid\n60\tdiagram draws only `B005 --> B006` for this epic. Every other gate edge is\n61\tdrawn (E001 → A003/B003, E004 → A005/B005, E007 → A008), so the omission makes\n62\tthe diagram incomplete relative to the frontmatter: a reader of the diagram\n63\talone would conclude BRANCH-B-E006 is gated only transitively through\n64\tBRANCH-B-E005. Per the cross-check rule (diagram edges must match frontmatter\n65\tdependencies and vice versa), this is a mismatch and scores 0. The fix is to\n66\tadd `E004 --> B006` to the diagram; the frontmatter itself is correct.\n67\t\n68\tEverything else in the DAG is sound: no cycles; no invalid edges (all\n69\tdependency targets exist); all six branch files set both `branch` and\n70\t`gated_by`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies\n71\t(BRANCH-B-E006's dependency on sibling BRANCH-B-E005 is within the same\n72\tbranch, which is correct); the E002 → E006 edge is justified under Rule 12\n73\t(the Dashboard's quota-consumption view consumes the per-project quota model\n74\tE002 establishes) and the remaining parallelism (E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E004 ∥ E007,\n75\tDashboard ∥ admission/preemption/topology tracks) is preserved; no\n76\tkonflux/license/repo-onboarding epics exist, so the mandated-parallelism rules\n77\tare not implicated. Critical path (main-plan 2, branch-inclusive 3) is within\n78\tthe expected range for an M-size strategy.\n79\t\n80\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n81\t\n82\tComponent/team tuples are consistent and correct: all Distributed Workloads\n83\tand Training Operator epics are owned by the Distributed Workloads team and\n84\tDashboard by RHOAI Dashboard, matching the architecture-context owning-team\n85\tfields (including Training Operator → Distributed Workloads). No epic spans\n86\tmultiple components or teams beyond same-logical-change work (BRANCH-B-E005\n87\tqueueing side + BRANCH-B-E006 training side are correctly split by component).\n88\tNo scope duplication across siblings: the E003 branches are mutually\n89\texclusive alternatives, not duplicate builds; quota (E002), admission\n90\t(branches E003), preemption (branches E005/E006), Dashboard (E006), and\n91\ttopology (BRANCH-A-E008) are disjoint. No work-product type mixing —\n92\tincidental documentation is folded as scope, with no content-authoring\n93\tbundled into implementation epics. No epic appears oversized.\n94\t\n95\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n96\t\n97\tAll three Investigations genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream\n98\tstructure: E001 selects between two implementation paths for HLR-1, E004\n99\tselects between paths for HLR-4 (and adds/removes the checkpoint-hooks epic),\n100\tE007 decides whether HLR-5 is built at all. All Implementations produce\n101\tconcrete artifacts. Gating metadata is complete and correct: every branch epic\n102\thas both `gate_failure_impact.action` and `fallback_approach` populated; every\n103\tepic listing an Investigation in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set; every\n104\t`gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` list.\n105\t\n106\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n107\t\n108\tType-appropriate signal sets throughout: all eight Implementation epics carry\n109\t`ai_signals` (9-signal rubric), all three Investigations carry\n110\t`investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), and no epic mixes them. All eleven\n111\tai-signals rationale files exist and their rationales match the frontmatter\n112\tvalues. Signal values are consistent with strategy content: E004 correctly\n113\tmarks `source_accessibility: 0` / `human_judgment_required: -2` (customer\n114\tpreference is a pending human decision, not a readable-source answer); the\n115\tbranch-B epics are scored appropriately low (greenfield integration, no\n116\tplatform precedent, likely upstream contribution for graceful preemption);\n117\tbranch-A epics score high on existing Kueue machinery. E001's\n118\t`cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` is borderline (the decision criterion\n119\tincludes \"at target scale\", which needs a live cluster) but the rationale\n120\texplicitly confronts this and the admission semantics are verifiable without\n121\tGPU hardware — defensible, not contradictory.\n122\t\n123\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n124\t\n125\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the HLRs. Both\n126\treplacement epics carry the rule-mandated rollback/feature-flag ACs:\n127\tBRANCH-A-E003 (\"admission semantics can be reverted per-queue via\n128\tconfiguration without platform reinstall\") and BRANCH-B-E003 (\"disabling the\n129\tplugin reverts to previous scheduling behavior without platform reinstall\").\n130\tNo konflux-chain or docs-authoring epics exist, so those mandated ACs do not\n131\tapply. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — investigations are\n132\treferenced by name throughout. Minor observation (not scored): the preemption\n133\tepics (BRANCH-A/B-E005) change scheduling behavior for existing workloads but\n134\trely on \"policy is admin-configurable\" as their disable path rather than an\n135\texplicit rollback AC; acceptable since preemption is a new capability, not a\n136\treplacement.\n137\t\n138\t### 7. Completeness (1/2)\n139\t\n140\tCore scope is fully covered: all five HLRs, all three open questions mapped to\n141\tInvestigations with recorded downstream effects, out-of-scope boundaries\n142\trespected (no MIG/fractioning, no multi-cluster), and strategy-level context\n143\t(Tech Preview risk, conflicting field signals, missing strategy sections)\n144\tcarried forward via health warnings and tiered delivery. No docs-authoring\n145\tepic is required — the strategy declares no documentation support (no\n146\tPrerequisites & Process Gates section; the Documentation component is\n147\tunaffected), which the summary documents explicitly.\n148\t\n149\t**Minor — uncovered branch edge case.** E004's first acceptance criterion\n150\tpermits a third outcome: \"(or a bounded hybrid, e.g. admin-selectable per\n151\tqueue)\". The conditional-branch structure models exactly two mutually\n152\texclusive outcomes (A = full eviction, B = graceful/checkpoint-aware); a\n153\thybrid decision has no corresponding branch plan — it would require both\n154\tbranch A and branch B implementations, which the branch-letter mechanism\n155\tcannot express. The strategy's own open question 3 is framed as a binary\n156\tchoice, so the fix is to tighten the AC to select outcome A or B (keeping any\n157\thybrid observations as advisory context in the decision document), or to add\n158\ta hybrid branch. Branches cover the primary outcomes but not this edge case,\n159\tso the criterion scores 1.\n160\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a2e7340d23a